<p><a href="https://dailynous.com/2025/07/29/to-philosophers-of-easy-virtue-guest-post/">Rails&#8217; post</a> was about the ethics of sexual relationships among people in academic philosophy, couched in terms of advice for how people could virtuously navigate this tricky terrain, or, as they put it, how to be &#8220;a strong player.&#8221;</p>
<p>It was a controversial but widely discussed entry in a <a href="https://dailynous.com/2025/08/19/summer-2025-guest-post-series-recap/">series of guest posts</a> last summer. Some people really appreciated it. Some absolutely hated it. It generated some offense, some reflection, and some amusing social media commentary.</p>
<p>Rails recently got in touch with me again about a follow-up post on flirting at conferences. The problem it takes up is this: If the optimal amount of flirting at a conference is not zero, but flirting at a conference is morally risky (with possible hazards for the flirter, the flirtee, and third parties), how should flirting at conferences happen? Their post is below.</p>
